  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "asymmetric-connectivity-brain-neural-5064",
  "summary": "Recent preprints dissect how dendritic inhibition and synaptic plasticity shape computation, from fly goal coding to covariance discrimination.",
  "synthesis": "The three preprints converge on inhibition and synaptic organisation as determinants of what a network computes. In Drosophila FC2, FlyWire connectome-based spiking and dynamical models indicate that inhibition normalises an externally set goal rather than selecting among goals. Dendrinet shows that functional synapse clusters emerge from learning on covariance discrimination tasks, with dendritic nonlinearities and plasticity supporting them. DendriNet, a trainable framework, compares shunting and additive E/I integration on dendritic trees and proposes a gain-load-alignment principle governing when branch-local shunting helps. The newer element is treating the form of inhibitory integration, whether normalisation or selection and shunting or additive, as a variable governing computation. The systems differ in scale and method, so the shared thread is conceptual rather than methodological.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-27"
}
